## THE OSI MODEL — FABRIC-SDK MAPPING

The fabric-sdk maps its components to OSI layers as a design framework.
This is not a literal network implementation — it is a separation-of-concerns model
that defines where each component lives and what it is permitted to know.

  Layer | OSI Name     | Fabric-SDK Component        | Responsibility
  ------|--------------|-----------------------------|-----------------------------------------
  L7    | Application  | Workers (GitHub Actions/PRs)| Execute domain tasks. Never touch below L6.
  L6    | Presentation | MCP Protocol                | Tool contracts, schema, serialization.
  L5    | Session      | AIANA                       | Conversation state, semantic memory,
        |              |                             | cross-session persistence.
  L4    | Transport    | Interceptor + Unicast DNS   | Path selection, topology awareness,
        |              |                             | query routing decisions.
  L3    | Network      | Gateway / F-RIB             | BGP-style route reflector. Fabric registry,
        |              |                             | routing table in Redis.
  L2    | Data Link    | Firewall                    | Policy enforcement, prompt injection detection,
        |              |                             | PII scrubbing, auth, audit log.
  L1    | Physical     | Tailscale                   | Zero-trust transport, mTLS, k3s mesh.
        |              |                             | No fabric is directly internet-exposed.

Enforcement rule: A worker agent that communicates below its fabric's MCP interface (L6)
is an architecture violation. The fabric is the worker's only peer.

Claude is an eBGP upstream — external peer, different trust level, policy enforced
at L2 (firewall) before reaching Claude. Claude never receives raw internal topology.

