## ROUTING DECISION RULES

### Rule 1 — Single-Domain Queries
  Query touches one knowledge domain → route to that specialist.
  Examples:
    "Is my UniFi Dream Machine Pro healthy?" → unifi-ops
    "What Proxmox VMs are running on pve-01?" → proxmox-ops
    "Show me Dependabot alerts for ry-ops/stackforge" → github-ops or git-steer-ops
    "What do I know about MetalLB from past sessions?" → aiana-ops

### Rule 2 — Infrastructure Correlation Queries
  Query crosses infrastructure layers → route to primary domain, pass to secondary.
  Examples:
    "Why is my k3s pod failing to start?" → k3s-ops (primary) + proxmox-ops (node health)
    "Is this Sandfly alert a known CVE?" → sandfly-ops → cve-ops (enrichment)
    "Is my Tailscale subnet router healthy?" → tailscale-ops + unifi-ops (physical layer)

### Rule 3 — Operational Sequences (Multi-Agent Pipelines)
  Task requires ordered execution across multiple specialists.
  Defined sequences below. Always execute in order. Pass output of each step
  as context input to the next step.

### Rule 4 — Memory-First
  ANY query that starts with "what have I done", "what did we decide", "past sessions",
  "last time", "remember when", or similar recall language → START with aiana-ops.
  Do not skip AIANA even if the domain specialist seems obvious.
  AIANA recall feeds context into whatever specialist runs next.

### Rule 5 — Default Route
  Query matches no prefix in F-RIB → aggregate all partial matches → route to Claude.
  Claude receives: original query + all partial context from fabric recall.
  Never send Claude a cold query if any fabric has even partial knowledge.

### Rule 6 — Security Incidents Always Escalate
  Any query involving "threat", "malware", "intrusion", "unauthorized", "compromised"
  → mandatory sequence: sandfly-ops → cve-ops → github-ops (alert PR) → aiana-ops (record)
  Do not skip steps. Do not let a single specialist handle a security incident alone.

